{"ClientSettings":{"Pn":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"P"},"Sc":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"H"},"Qz":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"T"},"Ap":true,"Mute":true,"Lad":"2026-07-11T00:00:00Z","Iotd":0,"Gwb":0,"Tns":0,"Dft":null,"Mvs":0,"Flt":0,"Imp":1,"Tobn":0},"MediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Sharks have patrolled the seas for more than 420 million years, and as apex predators, they help keep marine ecosystems in balance. The tiny explorer in today's image—a lemon shark pup weaving through a mangrove forest in Eleuthera, the Bahamas—offers a perfect reminder that their stories don't begin with drama but with nurseries and survival. Lemon sharks give birth to live young, and their pups spend years in shallow mangroves, where calm, sheltered waters provide a safe place to grow. As adults, they can reach about 3.4 metres in length and females often return to the same nursery sites to breed. Hunting mainly at night, they use specialised electroreceptors to detect prey hidden beneath sand or in murky water. They also benefit from living in groups, which can improve communication, courtship, hunting success and protection. Yet lemon sharks are considered near threatened, and sharks worldwide face growing pressure from overfishing, fear-driven killing and habitat loss.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.LemonShark_ROW1540729665_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.LemonShark_ROW1540729665_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Lemon shark pup in mangrove forest, Eleuthera, Bahamas","Copyright":"© Shane Gross/Nature Picture Library","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260710_LemonShark\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Lemon+shark&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260710_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260710_LemonShark"},"Ssd":"20260710","Name":"LemonShark","Market":"ROW","Hash":"1540729665","FullDateString":"10 juli 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Long before these terraces became famous, they were built for a simple purpose: growing rice. In Vietnam, generations of farmers gradually shaped Sapa's mountain slopes into productive fields. In Lào Cai province, these stepped terraces have supported local communities for centuries. Built and maintained by ethnic groups such as the Hmong, Dao, Giáy and Tày, the terraces are designed to hold rainwater as it flows downhill. This makes rice farming possible where flat land is scarce. The design also helps to reduce soil erosion and conserve water.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.SapaVietnam_ROW9241114244_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.SapaVietnam_ROW9241114244_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Rice fields at Sapa, Lào Cai, Vietnam","Copyright":"© Anujak Jaimook/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260709_SapaVietnam\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Lao+Cai+Vietnam&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260709_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260709_SapaVietnam"},"Ssd":"20260709","Name":"SapaVietnam","Market":"ROW","Hash":"9241114244","FullDateString":"9 juli 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Set in Guatemala's western highlands, Lake Atitlán fills a vast volcanic caldera. Its deep blue waters are framed by steep crater walls and the cone-shaped volcanoes Atitlán, Tolimán and San Pedro. Wooden piers stretch into the still lake, emphasising the contrast between its calm surface and the rugged terrain shaped by volcanic activity.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.LakeAtitlan_ROW9281108051_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.LakeAtitlan_ROW9281108051_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Sunrise at Lake Atitlán, Guatemala","Copyright":"© shayes17/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260708_LakeAtitlan\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Lake+Atitlan+Guatemala&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260708_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260708_LakeAtitlan"},"Ssd":"20260708","Name":"LakeAtitlan","Market":"ROW","Hash":"9281108051","FullDateString":"8 juli 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"A bill like a multitool and plumage full of colour—the plate-billed mountain toucan is one of the Andes' most distinctive birds. It lives in the humid mountain forests of Ecuador and southwestern Colombia, perfectly adapted to life high in the canopy.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.MountainToucanOrchids_ROW9033619580_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.MountainToucanOrchids_ROW9033619580_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Plate-billed mountain toucan with orchids, Ecuador","Copyright":"© Murray Cooper/Minden Pictures","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260707_MountainToucanOrchids\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Plate-billed+mountain+toucan&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260707_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260707_MountainToucanOrchids"},"Ssd":"20260707","Name":"MountainToucanOrchids","Market":"ROW","Hash":"9033619580","FullDateString":"7 juli 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Founded by Greek settlers from Corinth around 734 BCE, the Italian city of Syracuse, on the island of Sicily, grew into one of the ancient Mediterranean's great powers and is now part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site, 'Syracuse and the Rocky Necropolis of Pantalica.' It is forever linked to Archimedes, whose genius still shapes its identity, and to Plato, who came to Syracuse hoping philosophy might shape political life. That layered past still feels tangible at Castello Maniace, a seafront fortress built for Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II in the 13th century, which can still be admired today.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.SyracuseItaly_ROW8772538423_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.SyracuseItaly_ROW8772538423_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Syracuse at sunset, Sicily, Italy","Copyright":"© Balate Dorin/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260706_SyracuseItaly\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Syracuse+Sicily&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260706_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260706_SyracuseItaly"},"Ssd":"20260706","Name":"SyracuseItaly","Market":"ROW","Hash":"8772538423","FullDateString":"6 juli 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"In southeastern France, on the Valensole Plateau, summer arrives in waves of purple and blue. Valensole—one of the largest plateaus in the region—lies in the Verdon Regional Natural Park between the Durance and Asse valleys, near the Gorges du Verdon and its lakes. From mid-June to early July, depending on altitude and weather, lavender and lavandin bloom and transform the landscape into one of Provence's most recognisable sights. The air carries a sharp, dry and unmistakably Mediterranean scent.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.LavenderRows_ROW8253115987_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.LavenderRows_ROW8253115987_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Lavender rows, Plateau de Valensole, Provence, France","Copyright":"© Robert Harding/Shutterstock","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260705_LavenderRows\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Lavandula&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260705_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260705_LavenderRows"},"Ssd":"20260705","Name":"LavenderRows","Market":"ROW","Hash":"8253115987","FullDateString":"5 juli 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Tucked between vineyards and mountains, Kaysersberg is one of Alsace's most enchanting villages. Set along the Weiss River in northeastern France, it lies about 12 kilometres northwest of Colmar on the eastern slopes of the Vosges Mountains.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.KaysersbergVillage_ROW7990431342_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.KaysersbergVillage_ROW7990431342_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Kaysersberg, Alsace, France","Copyright":"© Federica Gentile/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260704_KaysersbergVillage\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Kaysersberg&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260704_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260704_KaysersbergVillage"},"Ssd":"20260704","Name":"KaysersbergVillage","Market":"ROW","Hash":"7990431342","FullDateString":"4 juli 2026"}],"PreloadMediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"On the remote northeastern tip of Crete, Greece, lies a quiet corner far from typical tourist routes. Today's image shows Vai Beach, a broad curve of golden sand bordered by calm, shallow waters of the Aegean Sea. With sunbeds, umbrellas and showers, it's ideal for swimming, relaxing or enjoying the peaceful atmosphere. But what truly sets Vai apart is just beyond the sand: Europe's largest natural palm grove. It consists of more than 5,000 Cretan date palm trees, a species native to Crete and parts of Turkey.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.VaiUmbrellas_ROW3620558002_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.VaiUmbrellas_ROW3620558002_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Blue sunbeds on a sandy beach in Vai, Crete, Greece","Copyright":"© borchee/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260717_VaiUmbrellas\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Crete+Greece&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260717_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260717_VaiUmbrellas"},"Ssd":"20260717","Name":"VaiUmbrellas","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3620558002","FullDateString":"17 juli 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Some places are built for a single purpose; others keep reinventing themselves. Navy Pier in Chicago, Illinois, United States, falls into the second category. The structure stretches more than 915 metres into the lake and was designed as part of Edward H. Bennett's and Daniel Burnham's 1909 Plan of Chicago. Opened in 1916, the pier was built for shipping, recreation and passenger travel on Lake Michigan. It was renamed Navy Pier in honour of World War I naval veterans.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.NavyPier_ROW3320288892_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.NavyPier_ROW3320288892_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Navy Pier, Chicago, Illinois, United States","Copyright":"© Christopher.F Photography/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260716_NavyPier\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Navy+Pier+Chicago&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260716_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260716_NavyPier"},"Ssd":"20260716","Name":"NavyPier","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3320288892","FullDateString":"16 juli 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"If mountains could keep a record of the past, the John Muir Wilderness near Bishop, California, would have plenty to share. Its valleys, lakes and granite formations reveal a history shaped over millions of years. Established in 1964, this vast protected area spans more than 2,630 square kilometres of the Sierra Nevada and is named after naturalist John Muir. Today, parts of the Pacific Crest Trail and John Muir Trail pass through the area, drawing hikers into one of the largest high-elevation wilderness regions in the contiguous United States.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.MarieLake_ROW3037907828_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.MarieLake_ROW3037907828_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Marie Lake, John Muir Wilderness near Bishop, California, United States","Copyright":"© Steve Dunleavy/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260715_MarieLake\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=John+Muir+Wilderness&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260715_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260715_MarieLake"},"Ssd":"20260715","Name":"MarieLake","Market":"ROW","Hash":"3037907828","FullDateString":"15 juli 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Victoria, a southeastern state of Australia, extends along more than 1,800 kilometres of coastline shaped by the Southern Ocean. From above, its beaches appear as sweeping bands of pale sand edged by turquoise waters and rugged cliffs. This dynamic interaction of land and sea defines much of the region's appeal. The famous Great Ocean Road traces roughly 240 kilometres of this shoreline, winding past popular surf spots, forested headlands and seaside towns.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.VictoriaBeach_ROW2787109728_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.VictoriaBeach_ROW2787109728_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Aerial view of land and ocean, Victoria, Australia","Copyright":"© Nearmap/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260714_VictoriaBeach\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Victoria+Australia&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260714_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260714_VictoriaBeach"},"Ssd":"20260714","Name":"VictoriaBeach","Market":"ROW","Hash":"2787109728","FullDateString":"14 juli 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"The geological building blocks beneath our feet encourage curiosity about how rocks shape the Earth's landscapes and history. Few places display that story as vividly as Antelope Canyon, a narrow slot canyon on Navajo Nation land, east of Page, Arizona. Carved from Navajo Sandstone by flash floods and erosion, its walls reveal how water and sediment sculpt rock over time.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.NavajoSandstone_ROW2504391338_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.NavajoSandstone_ROW2504391338_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Antelope Canyon on the Navajo Nation, east of Page, Arizona, United States","Copyright":"© Mark Skalny/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260713_NavajoSandstone\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Antelope+Canyon&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260713_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260713_NavajoSandstone"},"Ssd":"20260713","Name":"NavajoSandstone","Market":"ROW","Hash":"2504391338","FullDateString":"13 juli 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"If you're looking for Maine at its most untamed, this is it. Katahdin Woods and Waters National Monument spans more than 350 square kilometres of the rugged North Woods, where quiet ponds mirror the sky and dense forests seem to swallow sound. Here, the East Branch of the Penobscot River threads through forests and wetlands, adding to the sense of space and solitude that defines the North Woods.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.KatahdinWWNM_ROW0056133161_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.KatahdinWWNM_ROW0056133161_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Katahdin Woods and Waters National Monument, Maine, United States","Copyright":"© Cavan Images/Offset/Shutterstock","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20260712_KatahdinWWNM\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Katahdin+Woods+and+Waters+National+Monument&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20260712_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20260712_KatahdinWWNM"},"Ssd":"20260712","Name":"KatahdinWWNM","Market":"ROW","Hash":"0056133161","FullDateString":"12 juli 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Imagine a city built by animals, glowing in the sunlight and bustling with life beneath the waves. That is a coral reef. Coral reefs cover less than 1% of the ocean floor, yet they support roughly 25% of all marine life. What looks like colourful rocks are actually colonies of tiny animals called coral polyps. Together, they build vast limestone structures that can last for thousands of years. Stretching about 2,300 kilometres along Australia's coast, the Great Barrier Reef is the largest living structure on Earth.","Image":{"Url